    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65950",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- If we add $1$ to the first interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\le -1+1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\le 0 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If we add $1$ to the second interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\ge 1+1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1\\ge 2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le 0,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )"
            },
