    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-81567",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4.$",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- If we add $4$ to the first interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le -1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le 3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If we add $4$ to the second interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le 3,\\,f(x)\\ge 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$",
