    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-66060",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\ge 1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\le -1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 3,\\,f(x)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
