      "knowledge_point": "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "What is the value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1$ at its local maximum points?",
      "worked_solution": "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -4 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -4+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-3$ at the local maximum points.",
