      "knowledge_point": "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "What is the value of $f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3$ at its local minimum points?",
      "worked_solution": "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and adding $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & f(x)\\ge \\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{11}{3}$ at its local minimum points.",
