      "worked_solution": "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\frac{1}{2}$ and adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\le -\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}$ at the local maximum points.",
