    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-117803",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2$ at its local maximum points?",
      "worked_solution": "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are **local minimums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$  For now, we're interested in the local minimums (not local maximums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -3+2 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-1$ at its local maximum points.",
