    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65962",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+1)-2?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+1)-2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+1=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =-1+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}"
            },
