    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-66076",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x\\ne \\frac{4\\pi }{3}+5n\\pi ",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
