    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-66033",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and are given by the following expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
