    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-117809",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =4\\cdot {60}^{∘}+4\\cdot n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
