    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3860",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $y = -2\\csc(3x+5)-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\csc x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\csc x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup  [1, \\infty)\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right) \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup  [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\leq -1, \\qquad \\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\ge 2-1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\ge 1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\ge 1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\le -2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\le -2-1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\le -3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 1, \\qquad f(x) \\leq -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty, -3] \\cup [1, \\infty).$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3861",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "What is the value of $f(x) = 2\\csc\\left(x+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)$ at its local maximum points?",
      "worked_solution": "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\csc x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc x \\leq -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\csc x = -1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\csc x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(x+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right) \\leq -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\le -2 \\\\ f(x) & \\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x) = -2$ at the local maximum points.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3859",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Find an expression for the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = 2\\csc\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The asymptotes of $y=\\csc x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\sin x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $2\\csc\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} =n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x  =\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + n\\pi,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
